  [1mWhat changed from your original request, in one place:[0m

  - [38;5;153mlogin[39m does [1mnot[0m get a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter. Its signature stays [38;5;153m(username, password)[39m. A [38;5;153muserId[39m can't be passed [3min[0m because
    nothing knows it before authentication — it comes back [3mfrom[0m the server.
  - The compliance trail itself is [1mnot built here[0m. The browser can't produce evidence about who logged in, because the
    browser is the thing being audited. You scoped this to this repo, so the spec says explicitly that implementing all
    of it does not give you a compliance-grade audit trail — it makes the frontend ready to correlate with one that the
    backend still needs.
  - My self-review caught the design smuggling approach B back in: [38;5;153mapiRequest[39m was going to send the client-held [38;5;153muserId[39m,
    which would have put a client-supplied value into the audit path — the exact thing that got B rejected. Fixed: the
    client sends only a correlation id, and the server identifies the caller from its HttpOnly cookie.

